<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>
Selection-rate differences vs. within–element baselines, expressed as <math id="S4.T5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\%=(\mathrm{RR}-1)\times 100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>RR</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\%=(\mathrm{RR}-1)\times 100</annotation></semantics></math> (Poisson GEE, run–clustered; Wald <math id="S4.T5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>; offset <math id="S4.T5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\log K_{\mathrm{elem}}+\log n_{\text{items}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>elem</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>items</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\log K_{\mathrm{elem}}+\log n_{\text{items}}</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S4.T5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2{,}880" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2,880</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2{,}880</annotation></semantics></math> runs).
Only entries with <math id="S4.T5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.05</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.T5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta|\geq 50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta|\geq 50\%</annotation></semantics></math> are shown. A complete table with RRs under both offsets (<math id="S4.T5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log K</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.T5.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log K+\log n_{\mathrm{items}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>items</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log K+\log n_{\mathrm{items}}</annotation></semantics></math>) appears in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Category-Level Selection Rate Ratios vs. Within-Element Baseline Category ‣ Style Over Story: A Process-Oriented Study of Authorial Creativity in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix E</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.2 </span>Model Differences in Category Preference: Stable within <em id="S4.SS3.SSS2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Style</em>, Varied among <em id="S4.SS3.SSS2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Event</em>/<em id="S4.SS3.SSS2.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Character</em>/<em id="S4.SS3.SSS2.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Setting</em></h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Within the <em id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Style</em> element, models showed no clear preference across different categories. Combined with the element-level result that overall <em id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Style</em> selection differs across models (<a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2.2 Model Differences in Element Preference: Gpt4.1 as a Style-Dominant Outlier and a Boundary Marker ‣ 4.2 Element-Level Selection Patterns ‣ 4 Results ‣ Style Over Story: A Process-Oriented Study of Authorial Creativity in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a>), this implies that cross-model variation in style manifests as aggregate weighting of the element rather than distinct category preferences. By contrast, across the other elements—<em id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Event</em>, <em id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Character</em>, and <em id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Setting</em>—models exhibit varied differences and preferences across categories (<a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.3.2 Model Differences in Category Preference: Stable within Style, Varied among Event/Character/Setting ‣ 4.3 Category-Level Selection Patterns ‣ 4 Results ‣ Style Over Story: A Process-Oriented Study of Authorial Creativity in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 6</span></a>). Altogether, outside of <em id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Style</em> the models present category-specific profiles, whereas <em id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Style</em> functions chiefly as a shared control dimension with stable internal choices.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T6" class="ltx_table">
