<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>Prompt-type contrasts by category, showing only results significant after BH–FDR correction with <math id="S4.T7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta|\geq 50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta|\geq 50\%</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.T7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>). We report <math id="S4.T7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=(\mathrm{RR}-1)\times 100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>RR</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=(\mathrm{RR}-1)\times 100</annotation></semantics></math>; positive (negative) values indicate higher (lower) selection under the first-listed prompt. <em id="S4.T7.14" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Style</em> showed no contrasts significant after BH–FDR correction in any category. <em id="S4.T7.15" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Setting</em> had contrasts significant after BH–FDR correction, but none reached the <math id="S4.T7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta|\geq 50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta|\geq 50\%</annotation></semantics></math> threshold, so they are omitted. Values are rounded to the nearest integer.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Axis-Level Patterns</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on the constraint-level analysis, we identify significantly over- or under-selected constraints and then aggregate them to reveal systematic axis-level orientations (See <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Top-5 Over- or Under-selected axes by system prompt ‣ Style Over Story: A Process-Oriented Study of Authorial Creativity in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix G</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model &amp; test</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Within Experiment 2–2, we assess constraint-level over- or under-selection via an exact permutation test stratified by <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{model}\times\text{persona}\times\text{element}\times\text{category}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>model</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>persona</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>element</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>category</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{model}\times\text{persona}\times\text{element}\times\text{category}</annotation></semantics></math>. The null assumes exchangeability across constraints conditional on each run’s selection budget <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and pool composition. For each constraint <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the observed total <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{c}=\sum_{u}y_{uc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>u</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{c}=\sum_{u}y_{uc}</annotation></semantics></math> and the supply-adjusted expectation <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y_{c}]=\sum_{u}K_{u}\,(n_{c,u}/N_{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>u</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y_{c}]=\sum_{u}K_{u}\,(n_{c,u}/N_{u})</annotation></semantics></math>. We report <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{share}_{\mathrm{obs}}=Y_{c}/\sum_{u}K_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>share</mi><mi>obs</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>u</mi></msub><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{share}_{\mathrm{obs}}=Y_{c}/\sum_{u}K_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{share}_{\mathrm{exp}}=\mathbb{E}[Y_{c}]/\sum_{u}K_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>share</mi><mi>exp</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>u</mi></msub><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{share}_{\mathrm{exp}}=\mathbb{E}[Y_{c}]/\sum_{u}K_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{RD}_{\mathrm{share}}=\mathrm{share}_{\mathrm{obs}}-\mathrm{share}_{\mathrm{exp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>RD</mi><mi>share</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>share</mi><mi>obs</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>share</mi><mi>exp</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{RD}_{\mathrm{share}}=\mathrm{share}_{\mathrm{obs}}-\mathrm{share}_{\mathrm{exp}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a smoothed ratio <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{RR}_{\mathrm{obs/exp}}=(Y_{c}+0.5)/(\mathbb{E}[Y_{c}]+0.5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>RR</mi><mrow><mi>obs</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>exp</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{RR}_{\mathrm{obs/exp}}=(Y_{c}+0.5)/(\mathbb{E}[Y_{c}]+0.5)</annotation></semantics></math>; direction is defined by <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{share}_{\mathrm{obs}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>share</mi><mi>obs</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{share}_{\mathrm{obs}}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{share}_{\mathrm{exp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>share</mi><mi>exp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{share}_{\mathrm{exp}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Inference &amp; reporting</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Two-sided <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values (and one-sided <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{over}},p_{\text{under}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>over</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>under</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{over}},p_{\text{under}}</annotation></semantics></math>) come from <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B{=}2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B{=}2000</annotation></semantics></math> permutations with a +1 correction; multiplicity is controlled within stratum by Benjamini–Hochberg on <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{two}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>two</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{two}}</annotation></semantics></math> (significance at <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\leq.10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>.10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\leq.10</annotation></semantics></math>; fallback <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{two}}\leq.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>two</mtext></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{two}}\leq.05</annotation></semantics></math> in degenerate strata). Significant constraints are mapped to axis annotations and aggregated by <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\text{model}\times\text{system prompt}\times\text{direction})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>model</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>system prompt</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>direction</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\text{model}\times\text{system prompt}\times\text{direction})</annotation></semantics></math> to compute within-direction shares and enrichment relative to the global, direction-specific baseline; top axes are visualized with heatmaps.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.4.1 </span>Persona-Driven Axis-Level Preference: Creativity Avoids Realist Conditions</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 4.4.1 Persona-Driven Axis-Level Preference: Creativity Avoids Realist Conditions ‣ 4.4 Axis-Level Patterns ‣ 4 Results ‣ Style Over Story: A Process-Oriented Study of Authorial Creativity in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 8</span></a>, the axes where prompts diverge show a consistent realism break under Creativity. Compared to Basic and Quality, Creativity persona under-selects concrete, realist spatial scaffolds—<em id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Urban Built Environments</em>, <em id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Domestic Interior Spaces</em>, and <em id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Transit Hubs</em>—and likewise under-selects the presentist temporal frame indexed by <em id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">The Fully Connected Now</em>. By contrast, Basic and Quality over-select these same axes, indicating a preference for familiar, controllable world fixtures and contemporary temporality. This pattern is further supported by the fact that, across the full set of axes, Creativity’s top two preferences are <em id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Extraterrestrial Terrain</em> and <em id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Otherworldly or Mythic Realms</em>—both of which fall under the <em id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Macro spatial setting</em>—reinforcing its avoidance of realist spatial contexts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, Creativity uniquely over-selects <em id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Second person perspective</em>, while Basic and Quality under-select it. This suggests that Creativity persona expresses creativity by choosing <span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Second person perspective</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T8" class="ltx_table">
